-- 359bbdff
T["Automatic switching tries the enabled rotations in priority order, yours and Built Ins alike: higher numbers are tried first, equal priorities by name. It picks the first that matches; a rotation with no switch condition always matches, so turn it off to keep it from being chosen. The fallback (Default) is used only when none matches. The rotation list is sorted in this same order and shows each priority."] = 2422
-- 1adf9c9d
T["Priority sits beside Enabled at the top of the editor. A Built In cannot be edited, but its Enabled setting and priority are yours to change for this profile: they are saved at once, shown in amber when they differ from how it ships, and Reset to default puts it back. Copy a Built In to your rotations to edit anything else."] = 2423
-- 89d48b07
T["A Built In shows a blue Read-Only pill in place of Undo and Redo; Duplicate makes your own copy. It loads only for the edition and class it was written for (the pill's tooltip). The specializations it is for show in the picker before its name; any level it needs is part of its switch condition. The switch's tooltip says when it can be selected together with another rotation, or is for another specialization."] = 2424
-- c85105a0
T["In the list, a red border means the rotation cannot be selected (the tooltip names why), gray means it is switched off, and an amber bar means it can be selected at the same time as another rotation (yours or Built In), so only priority decides between them; a wider bar means they also have the same priority and the name decides. /rm why lists the order and what automatic switching would choose now."] = 2425
-- 929902f7
T["Each rotation says which specializations it is for, in the picker before its name: several, or Any. It is listed, in the minimap menu and for /rm set, only while you play one of them; its switch condition never hides it. Automatic switching checks the specialization first, then the switch condition, then priority; a rotation with no switch condition matches whenever its specialization and level apply, so priority alone decides; turn it off to keep it for choosing by hand. The specialization menu above the list's search box starts at the one you are playing; choose another, or All specializations, to see theirs. Where the game has dual specialization instead of named specializations (Classic Era, TBC, Wrath, Cataclysm and Forever), they are your primary and secondary talents: exports leave them out, and an import is for the talents the list shows."] = 2426
